<h1>卷首 Home</h1>

<p>
	Welcome to the <a href="https://github.com/ShiraTheMogul/fanyahanwen-corpus">泛亚汉文语料库 Fanya Hanwen Corpus</a>, a Literary Chinese corpus containing over twice as much text as Siku Quanshu! By employing a pan-Asian, diachronic perspective, the Fanya Hanwen Corpus hopes to propel the Literary Chinese tradition into the modern age.
</p>

<h2>存 Introduction</h2>
<p>
	The Fanya Hanwen Corpus contains texts from each Chinese dynasty, the Korean peninsula, Japan, Vietnam, and the Ryukyu Kingdom, with intent to cover Singapore and other regions in the future. Literary Chinese was and is a living language with a vibrant diversity in texts; it once enjoyed a similar status to European Latin during its heyday and should be treated as such. 
</p>

<p>
	The aim of this website is to make Literary Chinese more readable. In historical traditions, Literary Chinese was read with useful annotations: Not just kanbun and hanmun annotations and so on, but also markers for names, placenames, and so on, and more. These sorts of annotations are hard to come by on the modern internet but commonplace in, say, Qing dynasty texts. Additionally, viewing options such as vertical orientation from right-to-left, removing punctuation / annotation marks, Simplified Chinese, and so on, are also hard to come by. There are many components that are simply not easy to view and rarely all in one place. This site hopes to make this as easy as possible. 
</p>

<h2>字典 Dictionary</h2>
<p>
	This website contains a fairly robust dictionary that aims to be as inclusive as possible. At present, it contains Unihan data, Ruby text settings, ample phoneticization conventions, and the option to switch between Traditional and Simplified Chinese (as well as the original text in case of mix/var forms) whilst viewing things. Additionally, this database contains some variant form research derived from independent research, which hopes to map variants as exhaustively as possible in hopes of an eventual text normaliser. Please give feedback on this!
</p>

<p>
	Current dictionary definitions come from CC-CEDICT, Unihan, and Kangxi Dictionary. Kangxi Dictionary is regex'd to have linebreaks according to specific wording patterns in hopes of making it more readable, and so far it seems to have been successful. There are hopes to grab Shuowen Jiezi once a proper Oracle Bone Script font has been procured, as well as other historical dictionaries, periodic Wiktionary grabs, and other Public Domain sources, among others. These will also be used to annotate citations in these dictionaries. 
</p>

<h2>來想 Future Plans</h2>
<p>
	There are hopes to enable editing of this corpus to the public through an in-browser Pull Request system, which will be launched as the site backend improves. This will include edits to texts (correcting OCR/scribal errors, punctuation, etc), adding blurbs/synopses/summaries, and metadata details (authorship, time period, nation, etc). 
</p>